n":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"href":"https://link.springer.com/subjects/epigenomics","data-track":"select_related_subject_4","data-track-context":"related subjects from content page","data-track-label":"Epigenomics","data-track-type":"SNT"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Epigenomics"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n        \n            "},{"tag":"li","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"app-explore-related-subjects__items","data-test":"related-subject-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"href":"https://link.springer.com/subjects/methylation-analysis","data-track":"select_related_subject_5","data-track-context":"related subjects from content page","data-track-label":"Methylation analysis","data-track-type":"SNT"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Methylation analysis"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n        \n            "},{"tag":"li","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"app-explore-related-subjects__items","data-test":"related-subject-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"href":"https://link.springer.com/subjects/predictive-markers","data-track":"select_related_subject_6","data-track-context":"related subjects from content page","data-track-label":"Predictive markers","data-track-type":"SNT"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Predictive markers"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n        \n            "},{"tag":"li","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"app-explore-related-subjects__items","data-test":"related-subject-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"href":"https://link.springer.com/subjects/cellular-senescence-and-age-associated-mechanisms","data-track":"select_related_subject_7","data-track-context":"related subjects from content page","data-track-label":"Cellular Senescence and Age-Associated Mechanisms","data-track-type":"Citation topic"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Cellular Senescence and Age-Associated Mechanisms"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n        \n        "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n    "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n\n\n                    \n                        \n                                "},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"main-content"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                                    "},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Introduction"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec1-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"Sec1","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Introduction"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec1-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"It was demonstrated in 2013 by Horvath that probed DNA methylation levels at a subset of CpG sites in multiple tissues can be used as predictors of chronological age ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e394","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 1","title":"Horvath S. DNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types. Genome Biol. 2013;14:115.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. This was done by training a predictive model using elastic net (EN) regression, a penalized least squares regression which performs feature selection ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e397","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 2","title":"Zou H, Hastie T. Regularization and variable selection via the elastic net. J R Stat Soc Ser B Stat Methodol. 2005;67(2):301–20.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. The same year Hannum et al. published a similar model trained on DNA methylation samples in whole blood ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e400","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 3","title":"Hannum G, Guinney J, Zhao L, Zhang L, Hughes G, Sadda S, Klotzle B, Bibikova M, Fan J-B, Gao Y, Deconde R. Genomewide methylation profiles reveal quantitative views of human aging rates. Mol Cell. 2013;49(2):359–67.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. These chronological age predictors were dubbed DNA methylation (DNAm) clocks and have become colloquially known as the first-generation clocks."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"With little overlap between the CpG subsets selected by the Horvath multi-tissue and Hannum blood clocks and with the response variable in both clocks being known chronological age, support for the biological relevance of the clock predictions was lacking. The next generation of DNAm clocks was aimed at identifying subsets of CpGs which can predict biological age or measure biological age "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"acceleration"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":". While the precise methods and training datasets for each next-generation clock differ, they follow a general, two-stage construction: an initial filtering or feature "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"pre-selection"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" step whereby CpG probes which are correlates of some physiological or clinical metric of biological age are kept and the rest are discarded, and then using EN to both perform further feature selection from that smaller subset of CpG probes and to predict either age, time-to-death, or some other age surrogate from those selected features ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e412","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Levine ME, Lu AT, Quach A, Chen BH, Assimes TL, Bandinelli S, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Stewart JD, Li Y, Whitsel EA. An epigenetic biomarker of aging for lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2018;10(4):573–91.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e412_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Lu AT, Quach A, Wilson JG, Reiner AP, Aviv A, Raj K, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Li Y, Stewart JD, Whitsel EA. DNA methylation grimage strongly predicts lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2019;11(2):303–27.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR5"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e412_2","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Belsky DW, Caspi A, Corcoran DL, Sugden K, Poulton R, Arseneault L, Baccarelli A, Chamarti K, Gao X, Hannon E, Harrington H. Dunedinpace, a DNA methylation biomarker of the pace of aging. Elife. 2022;11:73420.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR6"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"6"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e415","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 7","title":"Ying K, Liu H, Tarkhov AE, Sadler MC, Lu AT, Moqri M, Horvath S, Kutalik Z, Shen X, Gladyshev VN. Causality-enriched epigenetic age uncouples damage and adaptation. Nat Aging. 2024;4:1–16.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR7"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"7"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"It has been shown that DNA methylation clocks—mathematical models with parameters optimized via machine learning—can yield generally accurate age predictions for humans and other mammals, usually by taking linear combinations of individual CpG probe measurements. When regressing model predictions on sample ages, the error, or residual, between the line of best fit and a subject’s actual chronological age has been proposed to indicate an acceleration of biological aging ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e421","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 1","title":"Horvath S. DNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types. Genome Biol. 2013;14:115.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e424","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Hannum G, Guinney J, Zhao L, Zhang L, Hughes G, Sadda S, Klotzle B, Bibikova M, Fan J-B, Gao Y, Deconde R. Genomewide methylation profiles reveal quantitative views of human aging rates. Mol Cell. 2013;49(2):359–67.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e424_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Levine ME, Lu AT, Quach A, Chen BH, Assimes TL, Bandinelli S, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Stewart JD, Li Y, Whitsel EA. An epigenetic biomarker of aging for lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2018;10(4):573–91.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e427","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 5","title":"Lu AT, Quach A, Wilson JG, Reiner AP, Aviv A, Raj K, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Li Y, Stewart JD, Whitsel EA. DNA methylation grimage strongly predicts lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2019;11(2):303–27.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR5"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e430","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Ying K, Liu H, Tarkhov AE, Sadler MC, Lu AT, Moqri M, Horvath S, Kutalik Z, Shen X, Gladyshev VN. Causality-enriched epigenetic age uncouples damage and adaptation. Nat Aging. 2024;4:1–16.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR7"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"7"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e430_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Horvath S, Raj K. DNA methylation-based biomarkers and the epigenetic clock theory of ageing. Nat Rev Genet. 2018;19(6):371–84.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR8"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"8"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e430_2","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bozack AK, Rifas-Shiman SL, Gold DR, Laubach ZM, Perng W, Hivert M-F, Cardenas A. DNA methylation age at birth and childhood: performance of epigenetic clocks and characteristics associated with epigenetic age acceleration in the project viva cohort. Clin Epigenetics. 2023;15(1):62.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR9"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"9"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e430_3","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bell CG, Lowe R, Adams PD, Baccarelli AA, Beck S, Bell JT, Christensen BC, Gladyshev VN, Heijmans BT, Horvath S, Ideker T. DNA methylation aging clocks: challenges and recommendations. Genome Biol. 2019;20:249.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR10"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"10"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e430_4","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"He X, Liu J, Liu B, Shi J. The use of DNA methylation clock in aging research. Exp Biol Med. 2021;246(4):436–46.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR11"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"11"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e433","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 12","title":"Bergsma T, Rogaeva E. DNA methylation clocks and their predictive capacity for aging phenotypes and healthspan. Neurosci Insights. 2020;15:2633105520942221.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR12"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"12"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"However, it remains unclear whether and to what degree such model variance is from random experimental variation and/or healthy epigenetic dynamics versus actual changes in health or biological aging. Epigenetic regulation of cell fates (myofiber vs. hepatocyte, vs. leukocyte, etc.) needs to be stable; in contrast, epigenetic dynamics is required for healthy cellular and tissue responses to their environments, changing with our daily routine, at night, and even with weather and financial status ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Stenvinkel P, Karimi M, Johansson S, Axelsson J, Suliman M, Lindholm B, Heimburger O, Barany P, Alvestrand A, Nordfors L, Qureshi AR. Impact of inflammation on epigenetic DNA methylation–a novel risk factor for cardiovascular disease? J Intern Med. 2007;261(5):488–99.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR13"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"13"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Kangaspeska S, Stride B, Metivier R, Polycarpou-Schwarz M, Ibberson D, Carmouche RP, Benes V, Gannon F, Reid G. Transient cyclical methylation of promoter DNA. Nature. 2008;452(7183):112–5.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR14"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"14"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_2","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Benbrahim-Tallaa L, Waterland RA, Styblo M, Achanzar WE, Webber MM, Waalkes MP. Molecular events associated with arsenic-induced malignant transformation of human prostatic epithelial cells: aberrant genomic DNA methylation and K-ras oncogene activation. Toxicol Appl Pharmacol. 2005;206(3):288–98.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR15"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"15"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_3","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bose R, Onishchenko N, Edoff K, Janson Lang AM, Ceccatelli S. Inherited effects of low-dose exposure to methylmercury in neural stem cells. Toxicol Sci. 2012;130(2):383–90.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR16"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"16"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_4","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Christiansen C, Castillo-Fernandez JE, Domingo-Relloso A, Zhao W, El-Sayed Moustafa JS, Tsai P-C, Maddock J, Haack K, Cole SA, Kardia SL, Molokhia M. Novel DNA methylation signatures of tobacco smoking with trans-ethnic effects. Clin Epigenetics. 2021;13:182.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR17"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"17"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_5","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Massart R, Freyburger M, Suderman M, Paquet J, El Helou J, Belanger-Nelson E, Rachalski A, Koumar OC, Carrier J, Szyf M, Mongrain V. The genome-wide landscape of DNA methylation and hydroxymethylation in response to sleep deprivation impacts on synaptic plasticity genes. Transl Psychiatry. 2014;4(1):347–347.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR18"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"18"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_6","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Cedernaes J, Osler ME, Voisin S, Broman JE, Vogel H, Dickson SL, Zierath JR, Schioth HB, Benedict C. Acute sleep loss induces tissue-specific epigenetic and transcriptional alterations to circadian clock genes in men. J Clin Endocrinol Metabol. 2015;100(9):1255–61.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR19"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"19"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_7","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bhatti P, Zhang Y, Song X, Makar KW, Sather CL, Kelsey KT, Houseman EA, Wang P. Nightshift work and genome-wide DNA methylation. Chronobiol Int. 2015;32(1):103–12.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR20"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"20"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e439_8","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bind MA, Zanobetti A, Gasparrini A, Peters A, Coull B, Baccarelli A, Tarantini L, Koutrakis P, Vokonas P, Schwartz J. Effects of temperature and relative humidity on DNA methylation. Epidemiology. 2014;25(4):561–9.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR21"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"21"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e442","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 22","title":"McDade TW, Ryan CP, Jones MJ, Hoke MK, Borja J, Miller GE, Kuzawa CW, Kobor MS. Genome-wide analysis of DNA methylation in relation to socioeconomic status during development and early adulthood. Am J Phys Anthropol. 2019;169(1):3–11.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR22"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"22"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. In out-of-sample data, the differences in healthy dynamics, as well as random and systematic errors, known as batch effects, and different data preprocessing, normalization, and other background corrections can all potentially lead to high residuals which would obviously not be attributable to age acceleration. Thus, a reliable metric of epigenetic shifts must be robustly above batch effect noise and normal, healthy dynamics to be broadly useful to clinicians and researchers."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Here, we expand the notion of the low accuracy and poor resolution between health and disease in linear DNAm clocks ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e449","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 23","title":"Mei X, Blanchard J, Luellen C, Conboy MJ, Conboy IM. Fail-tests of DNA methylation clocks, and development of a noise barometer for measuring epigenetic pressure of aging and disease. Aging (Albany NY). 2023;15(17):8552.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR23"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"23"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"], with novel findings that explain why ML-trained EN predictors underperform in these tasks. Furthermore, our results show that hypothesis-driven feature selection and interpretable ordinary least squares regression can effectively replace black-box machine learning methods and regularization for feature selection. Lastly, we discovered that counterproductive combinations of model weights and disease-related shifts in beta values create a net cancellation effect in a linear model. This causes what we term an "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"incoherence"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" effect, where the model fails to distinguish between healthy and patient populations despite actual differential methylation profiles between the two groups. To address this, we developed a method called "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"feature rectification"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", which produces a "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"coherent"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" shift effect between features, improving the capacity of linear models to capture methylome differences of aging in healthy vs. patient populations."}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Methods"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec2-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"Sec2","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Methods"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec2-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec3","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Dataset selection"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Our dataset selection was limited to publicly available datasets, specifically those published on the GEO database. We included datasets based on criteria ensuring relevance and quality: they needed to be conducted on whole blood or PBMC samples, include both disease and control cohorts of sufficient size, and provide sample age information. All selected datasets are associated with peer-reviewed publications, further supporting their quality and relevance. To ensure reproducibility and transparency, we have provided GEO accession numbers for each dataset used in our study."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec4","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Dataset preprocessing"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Preprocessing of raw IDAT files (when available) was performed in R version 4.3.1. All other data handling was performed in Python version 3.8.16. All statistical analyses were performed with the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"stats"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" module in the SciPy library, version 1.10.0. All linear modeling was performed with the relevant modules in the scikit-learn library, version 1.2.0."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Raw IDATs were preprocessed using the Minfi library. Noob preprocessing was applied to the entire dataset first and then BMIQ (wateRmelon library) quantile normalization."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Samples which had more than 5% missing values were removed from their respective datasets."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec5","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Imputation of missing values"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"KNNImputer (with k = 2) from the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"sklearn.impute"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" module was used to impute missing values."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec6","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Statistical analysis and measure of effect size"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Welch’s t-test for unequal variances or the non-parametric Mann–Whitney "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"U"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" test was performed for all hypothesis testing. Cohen’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" with pooled standard deviation was used to measure the effect size between sick cohorts and their corresponding healthy control cohorts:"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Equa","attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation__content"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d=\\frac{{\\overline{X} }_{2}-{\\overline{X} }_{1}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\left({n}_{1}-1\\right){\\upsigma }_{1}^{2}+\\left({n}_{2}-1\\right){\\upsigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{1}+{n}_{2}-2}}}"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]}]}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec7","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Analysis of the CpG selection of EN-trained DNAm clocks"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"All analyses of the CpG selection of first- and next-gen clock models were performed on the combined dataset (GSE42861, GSE125105, GSE106648, GSE72774)."}]},{"tag":"h4","hidden":false,"id":"Sec8","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Age correlation of individual CpG probes"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The healthy cohort of the combined dataset was used to measure the age correlation of each CpG probe in the Illumina HumanMethylation450 BeadChip array. The sample beta values for each CpG probe were regressed on the sample ages. The coefficient of determination ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"R"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-squared), standard error, and slope and intercept of the best-fit line was recorded for each regression."}]},{"tag":"h4","hidden":false,"id":"Sec9","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Normalized feature importance"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The normalized feature importance for the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"i"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"th CpG probe in a given model’s CpG probe selection was computed by the following:"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Equb","attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation__content"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"{\\text{Importance}}_{\\text{i}}=\\frac{|{\\text{weight}}_{\\text{i}}|/{\\text{stderr}}_{\\text{i}}}{|{\\text{weight}}_{\\text{max}}|/{\\text{stderr}}_{\\text{max}}}"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"where stderr"},{"tag":"sub","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"i"}]}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" is the standard error of the regression of the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"i"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"th CpG probe when its beta values are regressed on age."}]},{"tag":"h4","hidden":false,"id":"Sec10","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Age-dependent CpG probe variance"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The slope and intercept for the best-fit line of a probe’s age correlation give a sample’s predicted beta value "},{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\\widehat{\\beta }"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" by the following:"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Equc","attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation__content"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\\widehat{\\beta }={\\text{slope}}*{\\text{age}}+{\\text{intercept}}"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The residual for a sample for a given probe was calculated as follows:"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Equd","attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation__content"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"{\\text{residual}}=\\beta -\\widehat{\\beta }"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The absolute value of the residuals for a probe was then regressed on age."}]},{"tag":"h4","hidden":false,"id":"Sec11","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Disease-associated differential methylation of CpG probes"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"For each CpG probe, the Mann–Whitney "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"U"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" test was performed on the beta values between the healthy control and patient cohorts of the combined dataset, with "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"H"}]},{"tag":"sub","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"0"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" being there is no difference between their distributions."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec12","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Generating published model predictions"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The age predictions and PACE scores for the combined dataset were calculated by taking the dot product of sample beta values and the model weights provided in the supplementary files for the relevant models."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"For the Horvath clock predictions, the inverse of the log transform described in ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e925","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 1","title":"Horvath S. DNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types. Genome Biol. 2013;14:115.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"] was applied to the dot product to generate the final age predictions."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec13","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Average signed distance between regression lines"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The slope "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"m"}]},{"tag":"sub","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"A"}]}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" and intercept "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"y"}]},{"tag":"sub","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":",A"}]}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" for a best-fit line were used to generate "},{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"{\\widehat{{\\varvec{y}}}}_{A}={m}_{A}{\\varvec{x}}+{y}_{0,A}{1}_{100}"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]},{"tag":"#text","text":". Next,"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Eque","attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation__content"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"{D}_{\\left(A,B\\right)}=\\frac{1}{100}{\\sum }_{i}{\\widehat{y}}_{A,i}-{\\widehat{y}}_{B,i}"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Note that "},{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"{D}_{\\left(A,B\\right)}=-{D}_{\\left(B,A\\right)}"}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec14","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Effect size between regression lines"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The average distance between lines was calculated as described above. This average distance was divided by the pooled standard error of the two regression lines."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec15","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"DNAm clock prediction variance"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The ages for the healthy control and disease cohorts of the combined dataset were predicted for a given model by taking a linear combination of the beta values for the model’s selected CpG probes and their model weights. Next, the residuals were separately calculated, and the normality of their distribution was tested by the quantile–quantile plot. A normal distribution was then fitted to the residuals using the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"stats.norm.fit()"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" method from the SciPy library."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec16","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Feature rectification for model coherence"}]},{"tag":"h4","hidden":false,"id":"Sec17","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Calculating signal shifts for a disease"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Datasets containing healthy control "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"H"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" and patient "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"D"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" cohorts were split accordingly. Each CpG was individually regressed on age in the separate cohorts. The average signed distance "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"D"}]},{"tag":"sub","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"i"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"D,H"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":")"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" for the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"i"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"th CpG between the best-fit lines of the healthy and patient cohorts was calculated as described above."}]},{"tag":"h4","hidden":false,"id":"Sec18","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Reflection transformation"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The beta values for the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"i"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"th CpG in the training data for a disease-rectified model were transformed by the following:"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Equf","attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-equation__content"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"mathjax-tex"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"MathJax_Preview"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"{\\widetilde{\\beta }}_{i}=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll}{\\beta }_{i}& \\text{if }{D}_{i\\left(D,H\\right)}\\ge 0\\\\ 1-{\\beta }_{i}& \\text{if }{D}_{i\\left(D,H\\right)}<0\\end{array}\\right."}]},{"tag":"script","hidden":true}]}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Results"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec23-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"Sec23","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Results"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec23-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec24","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Analysis of elastic net CpG selection"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The elastic net performs feature selection via the L1 norm and minimizes the cost function over the training data ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1383","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 2","title":"Zou H, Hastie T. Regularization and variable selection via the elastic net. J R Stat Soc Ser B Stat Methodol. 2005;67(2):301–20.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. However, in biology, the focus is not the numerical value of a minimized cost function (e.g., − 42.42), but rather the specific CpG biomarkers selected and their age-related changes that influence gene expression. Therefore, we asked three aging-relevant questions: 1) whether a CpG probe’s correlation with chronological age was related to its importance in the model, 2) if EN selects CpGs based on an increased, age-related dysregulation, and 3) the possibility that the elastic net selects CpGs that are associated with inflammaging."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"These studies were applied to all major current clocks: first-generation Horvath multi-tissue and Hannum blood age predictors and the next-generation predictors, PhenoAge, AdaptAge, DamAge, and CausAge. Additionally, we analyzed the GpG probe selection for DunedinPACE, which is a putative measurement of the rate of biological aging. For our analysis we created a composite dataset (GSE42861, GSE125105, GSE72774, GSE106648) with datasets where DNA methylation was probed from either whole blood or PBMCs from healthy controls and patients suffering from diseases that are associated with inflammaging, such as rheumatoid arthritis, Parkinson’s disease, depression, and multiple sclerosis ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1389","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Santos-Moreno P, Burgos-Angulo G, Martinez-Ceballos MA, Pizano A, Echeverri D, Bautista-Nino PK, Roks AJ, Rojas-Villarraga A. Inflammaging as a link between autoimmunity and cardiovascular disease: the case of rheumatoid arthritis. RMD Open. 2021;7(1): 001470.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR24"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"24"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1389_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Rezus E, Cardoneanu A, Burlui A, Luca A, Codreanu C, Tamba BI, Stanciu G-D, Dima N, Badescu C, Rezus C. The link between inflammaging and degenerative joint diseases. Int J Mol Sci. 2019;20(3):614.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR25"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"25"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1389_2","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Calabrese V, Santoro A, Monti D, Crupi R, Di Paola R, Latteri S, Cuzzocrea S, Zappia M, Giordano J, Calabrese EJ, et al. Aging and Parkinson’s disease: inflammaging, neuroinflammation and biological remodeling as key factors in pathogenesis. Free Radical Biol Med. 2018;115:80–91.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR26"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"26"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1389_3","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Russo T, Riessland M. Age-related midbrain inflammation and senescence in Parkinson’s disease. Front Aging Neurosci. 2022;14: 917797.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR27"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"27"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1389_4","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Perdaens O, Van Pesch V. Molecular mechanisms of immunosenescene and inflammaging: relevance to the immunopathogenesis and treatment of multiple sclerosis. Front Neurol. 2022;12: 811518.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR28"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"28"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1389_5","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Deleidi M, Jaggle M, Rubino G. Immune aging, dysmetabolism, and inflammation in neurological diseases. Front Neurosci. 2015;9: 140325.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR29"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"29"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1392","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 30","title":"Simon MS, Schiweck C, Arteaga-Henriquez G, Poletti S, Haarman BC, Dik WA, Schwarz M, Vrieze E, Mikova O, Joergens S, et al. Monocyte mitochondrial dysfunction, inflammaging, and inflammatory pyroptosis in major depression. Prog Neuropsychopharmacol Biol Psychiatry. 2021;111: 110391.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR30"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"30"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. We define inflammaging as any disease being linked to persistent inflammation within one or more tissues, the risk for which increases with aging."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"First, we tested for correlation between a CpG’s importance in a model and the change in its beta values with age. Normalized feature importances were calculated for each CpG of each DNAm clock (methods), with an importance near zero indicating low importance, and a value of one indicating the most influential CpG in the model. For each model, we regressed the beta values of each CpG individually on the chronological ages of the healthy controls and plotted its "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"R"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-squared value against its relative importance (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a). Interestingly, in contradiction to the notion of predicting aging from the changes in the methylome, the most important CpGs were, in all models, those unchanged or changed the least in their methylation with age. Given that there are probes for which |"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"r"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"|> 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"8 for their age correlation (e.g., cg16867657), these results suggest that correlation of CpG methylation with age is not the primary criterion for its importance in a model, otherwise such CpGs would tend to be over-represented in EN models, and with higher importances than CpGs with lower age correlations."}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-1","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item","data-test":"figure","data-container-section":"figure","data-title":"Fig. 1"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Fig1","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-caption","data-test":"figure-caption-text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Fig. 1"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-content"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-item"},"children":[{"tag":"picture","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-picture"},"children":[{"tag":"source","hidden":false,"attributes":{"type":"image/webp","srcset":"//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig1_HTML.png?as=webp"}},{"tag":"img","hidden":false,"attributes":{"aria-describedby":"figure-1-desc","src":"https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig1_HTML.png","alt":"Fig. 1","loading":"lazy","width":"685","height":"191"}}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-link"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"article-link","data-track":"click","data-track-label":"button","data-track-action":"view figure","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/figures/1","data-track-dest":"link:Figure1 Full size image","aria-label":"Full size image figure 1","rel":"nofollow"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size image"}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-1-desc","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-description","data-test":"bottom-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"In all tested models, the importance of clock’s CpGs does not positively correlate with the changes in DNAm. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"a"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"R"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-squared of the age correlation of a feature vs. its normalized importance in the respective model. Color-coded dashed lines represent the mean "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"R"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-squared for a model’s features’ age correlations. The probes with the highest importance for all models had |"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"r"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"|< 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"45. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"b"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"R"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-squared of the residuals of a feature’s age correlation vs. its normalized importance in the respective model. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"c"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Negative log of a feature’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-value ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"H"}]},{"tag":"sub","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"0"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":": = no difference in distributions of beta values between controls and samples with inflammaging) vs. its normalized importance in the respective model"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Next, following up on our discovery that DNAm noise is a biomarker of aging ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e1463","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 23","title":"Mei X, Blanchard J, Luellen C, Conboy MJ, Conboy IM. Fail-tests of DNA methylation clocks, and development of a noise barometer for measuring epigenetic pressure of aging and disease. Aging (Albany NY). 2023;15(17):8552.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR23"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"23"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"], we explored the relationship between a selected CpG’s increase in variance with age and its relative importance (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b). To measure age-dependent variance, we took the residuals from the age correlations for each CpG and regressed them on chronological age. We then plotted the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"R"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-squared value for this regression against the CpG’s relative importance. Once again, we found a trend of decreasing correlation between age-specific CpG dysregulation and its importance in the EN model: features with the highest noise (|"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"r"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"|≤ 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"4) had the lowest importance, while the CpGs with the highest importance for each model tended to have low noise (|"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"r"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"|≤ 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"24). This suggests that age-related noise is not the primary criterion for feature importance in EN clocks."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Next, we explored the possibility that the elastic net selects CpGs which are significantly different between healthy people and patients with diseases associated with inflammaging (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"c). To do this, we performed a two-tailed Mann–Whitney "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"U"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" test (methods) on the beta value distributions of each CpG probe between healthy controls and patients. We then plotted the negative log of the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-value against the relative importance of each CpG in its respective model. Higher negative log values indicate larger differences between patients and healthy controls for that CpG probe. Again, as with the lack of CpG relevance to age-specific changes in values or dysregulation, there was a general trend of sharply increasing EN-assigned importance of CpGs that differ "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"the least"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" between healthy people and patients, indicating that the elastic net does not select CpGs based on their differential methylation due to inflammaging."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Thus, all examined clocks not only fail to weight DNAm biomarkers of aging by significance but also appear to preferentially exclude such biomarkers. The exclusion of biologically relevant CpGs results from the combination of the L1 penalty for feature selection and regularization, as well as minimizing the EN cost function. This function intentionally penalizes CpGs with little-to-no overall individual correlation with age or its proxies, even if they may correlate differently between various training cohorts (e.g., healthy control versus patient samples), by reducing their weights to zero (Box 1). The larger the signal shift between two cohorts in the training data at a CpG probe, the more likely EN is to assign a lower weight to that CpG. However, CpG sites with high degrees of differential methylation are often of the greatest biological interest, as they may indicate key differences related to inflammaging and, consequently, biological age acceleration.\n"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"table-1","attributes":{"class":"c-article-table","data-test":"inline-table","data-container-section":"table"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-table__figcaption"},"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Taba","attributes":{"data-test":"table-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Box 1 Demonstration: The elastic net may select against differentially methylated CpGs within the training set"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"u-text-right u-hide-print"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"table-link","data-track":"click","data-track-action":"view table","data-track-label":"button","rel":"nofollow","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/tables/1","aria-label":"Full size table 1"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size table"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec25","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Quantifying normal variance in DNAm clock residuals"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"One method for predicting biological age with DNAm clocks is to use the residual or difference between sample’s predicted age and their known chronological age, with deviations suggested to indicate age acceleration ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4808","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 1","title":"Horvath S. DNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types. Genome Biol. 2013;14:115.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4811","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Hannum G, Guinney J, Zhao L, Zhang L, Hughes G, Sadda S, Klotzle B, Bibikova M, Fan J-B, Gao Y, Deconde R. Genomewide methylation profiles reveal quantitative views of human aging rates. Mol Cell. 2013;49(2):359–67.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4811_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Levine ME, Lu AT, Quach A, Chen BH, Assimes TL, Bandinelli S, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Stewart JD, Li Y, Whitsel EA. An epigenetic biomarker of aging for lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2018;10(4):573–91.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4814","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 5","title":"Lu AT, Quach A, Wilson JG, Reiner AP, Aviv A, Raj K, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Li Y, Stewart JD, Whitsel EA. DNA methylation grimage strongly predicts lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2019;11(2):303–27.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR5"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4817","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Ying K, Liu H, Tarkhov AE, Sadler MC, Lu AT, Moqri M, Horvath S, Kutalik Z, Shen X, Gladyshev VN. Causality-enriched epigenetic age uncouples damage and adaptation. Nat Aging. 2024;4:1–16.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR7"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"7"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4817_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Horvath S, Raj K. DNA methylation-based biomarkers and the epigenetic clock theory of ageing. Nat Rev Genet. 2018;19(6):371–84.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR8"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"8"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4817_2","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bozack AK, Rifas-Shiman SL, Gold DR, Laubach ZM, Perng W, Hivert M-F, Cardenas A. DNA methylation age at birth and childhood: performance of epigenetic clocks and characteristics associated with epigenetic age acceleration in the project viva cohort. Clin Epigenetics. 2023;15(1):62.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR9"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"9"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4817_3","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bell CG, Lowe R, Adams PD, Baccarelli AA, Beck S, Bell JT, Christensen BC, Gladyshev VN, Heijmans BT, Horvath S, Ideker T. DNA methylation aging clocks: challenges and recommendations. Genome Biol. 2019;20:249.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR10"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"10"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4817_4","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"He X, Liu J, Liu B, Shi J. The use of DNA methylation clock in aging research. Exp Biol Med. 2021;246(4):436–46.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR11"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"11"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e4820","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 12","title":"Bergsma T, Rogaeva E. DNA methylation clocks and their predictive capacity for aging phenotypes and healthspan. Neurosci Insights. 2020;15:2633105520942221.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR12"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"12"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. However, residuals would be expected to not only include biological age acceleration, but also natural variation in the healthy human methylome as well as random experimental variation during the assays, data processing, and variance due to approximating a non-linear process with a linear model."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"To quantify normal variation and determine thresholds for potentially aberrant methylomes, we fit a probability distribution to the residuals of predicted ages for healthy controls from the composite dataset (GSE42861, GSE125105, GSE72774, GSE106648) for each predictive model. The residuals were approximately normally distributed according to Q-Q plots (Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a). Thus, a normal distribution was fit to the residuals for each model (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a). We also investigated the residual distributions at 10-year age intervals for each model (Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b)."}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-2","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item","data-test":"figure","data-container-section":"figure","data-title":"Fig. 2"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Fig2","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-caption","data-test":"figure-caption-text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Fig. 2"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-content"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-item"},"children":[{"tag":"picture","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-picture"},"children":[{"tag":"source","hidden":false,"attributes":{"type":"image/webp","srcset":"//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig2_HTML.png?as=webp"}},{"tag":"img","hidden":false,"attributes":{"aria-describedby":"figure-2-desc","src":"https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig2_HTML.png","alt":"Fig. 2","loading":"lazy","width":"685","height":"375"}}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-link"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"article-link","data-track":"click","data-track-label":"button","data-track-action":"view figure","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/figures/2","data-track-dest":"link:Figure2 Full size image","aria-label":"Full size image figure 2","rel":"nofollow"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size image"}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-2-desc","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-description","data-test":"bottom-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Normal variance in predictions of biological age for healthy people is much larger than the residuals of the predictions for the inflammaging samples. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"a"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Fitted normal distribution for the residuals of first- and next-generation clocks on healthy controls in the composite dataset. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"b"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Fitted normal distributions for the residuals of clocks on the inflammaging cohort in the composite dataset. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"c"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" KDE plots with histograms normalized by cohort size comparing the distributions of residuals for the healthy control cohort (blue) and inflammaging cohort (red) in the composite dataset for first- and next-generation clocks ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":": = Cohen’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"). "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" DunedinPACE score calculated for the healthy control and inflammaging cohorts vs. chronological age. Blue and red dashed best-fit lines are for the healthy and inflammaging cohorts, respectively. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"e"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Normalized KDE plots and histograms comparing the distributions of PACE scores for the healthy and inflammaging cohorts. Corresponding dashed lines represent cohort means"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Next, we calculated the 95% interpercentile range (95-IPR, i.e., the range containing the central 95% of residuals) for the healthy cohort (HC) for each model. Additionally, we calculated the means of the residuals of the healthy cohort and inflammaging cohort (IC) to quantify how well-calibrated each model is to test data. We define good calibration as having a residual mean close to zero, indicating there is no bias for having more positive or more negative residuals. These results, summarized in Table "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"table anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Tab1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", demonstrate that, when correcting for the mean shift, residuals of up to 10.8 years fall within the expected range for healthy individuals for the model with the smallest 95-IPR and thus the highest precision (Hannum). Averaging over all models tested, a residual of over 24.6 years would be needed to be outside the expected range for healthy individuals, when correcting for mean shifts. The mean shifts for the inflammaging cohort for all models are less than this threshold by an order of magnitude, suggesting that all models lack sufficient power to detect the age-accelerating effects of inflammaging (Table "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"table anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Tab1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b).\n"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"table-2","attributes":{"class":"c-article-table","data-test":"inline-table","data-container-section":"table"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-table__figcaption"},"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Tab1","attributes":{"data-test":"table-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Table 1 Residual distribution and calibration of DNAm clocks"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"u-text-right u-hide-print"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"table-link","data-track":"click","data-track-action":"view table","data-track-label":"button","rel":"nofollow","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/tables/2","aria-label":"Full size table 2"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size table"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"We performed a two-tailed Welch’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"t"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-test to determine statistical significance between the distributions of residuals for the healthy and inflammaging cohorts (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"c). Importantly, high numbers of samples can yield artificially low "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-values so we also quantified effect size, which is more indicative of the power of a model. To this end, we used Cohen’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", which is a signal-to-noise ratio, and we used the effect size rule of thumb suggested by Sawilowsky (2009) ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5094","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 32","title":"Sawilowsky SS. New effect size rules of thumb. J Mod Appl Stat Methods. 2009;8:597–9.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR32"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"32"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. Of the models, only PhenoAge and AdaptAge had statistically "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"higher"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" means of the residuals ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" < 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"05) for patients, but both have small effect sizes. Moreover, the higher mean shifts for the inflammaging cohort were well within the 95-IPR for both models. These results suggest that all the models investigated lack sufficient power to detect the age-accelerating effects of inflammaging via residual."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Next, we analyzed the DunedinPACE model separately as it yields a score rather than a predicted age, with a higher score indicating a higher rate, or pace of aging ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5111","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 6","title":"Belsky DW, Caspi A, Corcoran DL, Sugden K, Poulton R, Arseneault L, Baccarelli A, Chamarti K, Gao X, Hannon E, Harrington H. Dunedinpace, a DNA methylation biomarker of the pace of aging. Elife. 2022;11:73420.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR6"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"6"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. We first generated a PACE score (methods) for each sample in both the healthy and patient cohorts of the composite dataset and plotted the PACE score versus chronological age for both (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"d). PACE was trained on samples from people who were all ∼53 years, yet interestingly, it worked as an age predictor when tested on the combined dataset that spans 20–90 + yrs. This observation demonstrates that training predictors that are proxies of chronology, e.g., instances of disease increase with aging, continue to predict age."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Next, to determine if PACE detects inflammaging, we performed a two-tailed Welch’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"t"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-test, comparing the means of the distributions between the cohorts and calculating the effect size (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"e). While the mean of the inflammaging population slightly shifted upwards with "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"38, this is a small effect size for inflammaging, a hallmark of aging that should be robustly discernable."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Summarily, most models failed in distinguishing health from inflammaging, and even PACE, which was trained on extreme differences between people who were healthy vs. those who had heart attacks, strokes, etc., had only a marginal power to detect the age-accelerating effects of inflammaging. Overall, there remains room for improvement in feature selection."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec26","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"OLS regression suffices for a generalizable age predictor with hypothesis-driven feature selection"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"DNAm clocks trained on DNA methylation array datasets typically include tens to hundreds of thousands of CpG features ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":") and hundreds to a few thousand samples ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"n"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"). Ordinary least squares regression has the advantage of good interpretability on the correlations between the response and predictor variables, but on this structure of data, where "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" > > "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"n"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", OLS tends to have significant overfitting issues and has very high variance on out-of-sample data ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5155","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 2","title":"Zou H, Hastie T. Regularization and variable selection via the elastic net. J R Stat Soc Ser B Stat Methodol. 2005;67(2):301–20.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. Elastic net regression models combat this issue via regularization, which performs feature selection and introduces bias to the model, effectively reducing variance on out-of-sample data. This makes EN more generalizable than ordinary least squares models. However, as demonstrated, EN clock models suffer a lack of biological interpretability and may select against features with the most biological significance."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"We hypothesized that, due to the presence of many CpGs with high linear age correlations, it is possible to construct a generalizable and interpretable simple ordinary least squares age predictor from a reduced set of CpGs that have a priori biological relevance. To test this hypothesis, we implemented a modified version of the forward stepwise selection (mFSS) algorithm (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a, Algorithm 1)."}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-3","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item","data-test":"figure","data-container-section":"figure","data-title":"Fig. 3"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Fig3","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-caption","data-test":"figure-caption-text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Fig. 3"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-content"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-item"},"children":[{"tag":"picture","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-picture"},"children":[{"tag":"source","hidden":false,"attributes":{"type":"image/webp","srcset":"//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig3_HTML.png?as=webp"}},{"tag":"img","hidden":false,"attributes":{"aria-describedby":"figure-3-desc","src":"https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig3_HTML.png","alt":"Fig. 3","loading":"lazy","width":"685","height":"419"}}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-link"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"article-link","data-track":"click","data-track-label":"button","data-track-action":"view figure","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/figures/3","data-track-dest":"link:Figure3 Full size image","aria-label":"Full size image figure 3","rel":"nofollow"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size image"}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-3-desc","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-description","data-test":"bottom-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Hypothesis-driven mFSS OLS model. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"a"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Schema of the conventional forward stepwise selection and the modified forward stepwise selection algorithms. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"b"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" (Top) Pearson correlation vs. number of features included in the model. (Bottom) Mean squared error vs. number of features included in the model. Vertical dashed red line indicates the optimum number of features. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"c"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" The optimum mFSS model predictions on the training set (GSE40279). Black dashed line is "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"y"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"x"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":". "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" mFSS model predictions on the test set (GSE55763). "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"e"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" mFSS model predictions on out-of-sample test data with rheumatoid arthritis patients and healthy controls (GSE42861). "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"f"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" mFSS model predictions on out-of-sample test data with depression patients and healthy controls (GSE125105). "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"g"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" mFSS model predictions on out-of-sample test data with multiple sclerosis patients and healthy controls (GSE106648). "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"h"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" mFSS model predictions on out-of-sample test data with Parkinson’s disease patients and healthy controls (GSE72774)"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-a","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item","data-test":"figure","data-container-section":"figure","data-title":"Algorithm 1"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Figa","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-caption","data-test":"figure-caption-text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Algorithm 1"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-content"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-item"},"children":[{"tag":"picture","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-picture"},"children":[{"tag":"img","hidden":false,"attributes":{"aria-describedby":"figure-a-desc","src":"https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Figa_HTML.png","alt":"Algorithm 1","loading":"lazy","width":"685","height":"620"}}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-link"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"article-link","data-track":"click","data-track-label":"button","data-track-action":"view figure","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/figures/a","data-track-dest":"link:Figurea Full size image","aria-label":"Full size image figure a","rel":"nofollow"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size image"}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-a-desc","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-description","data-test":"bottom-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"modified Forward Stepwise Selection (mFSS)"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Briefly, we begin with a list of features, ranked by some biological criterion (e.g., correlation with age, increase in noise with age). An OLS regression model is trained as each feature is sequentially added to the model, recording the value of a cost function (e.g., mean squared error) on a test set at each iteration. If the addition of a feature achieves a new low for the cost function, we record that model as the best model. We employ a patience hyperparameter "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"P"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" which allows for up to "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"P"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" unproductive feature additions (i.e., producing a model with a new minimum cost) before stopping and returning the last best model with its optimal feature selection. The algorithm stops either after "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"P"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" unproductive additions or after all features in the initial list have been added."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"We generated a ranked list of features from a 450K DNAm array dataset (GSE40279, "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"N"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 656) by regressing chronological age on each CpG feature independently, ranking the CpGs by the magnitude of their age correlation. This same dataset was used for training, and we used an out-of-sample dataset (GSE55763, "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"N"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 2631) as a test set. We next trained an mFSS OLS model, splitting the training data (85/15) into training and validation sets (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a, b). The selected features and weights for this model are in Supplementary Excel "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM5"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":". The top-ranked CpG alone yields a model with "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"r"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" > 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"8, and the top 74 CpGs yielded the best OLS model with "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"r"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"9 and mean absolute error (MAE) of 3.7 years in the test set (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b–d). The mFSS clock displays high accuracy and low variance on out-of-sample data, demonstrating that it is generalizable and does not suffer from overfitting (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"e–h). Importantly, the mFSS clock trained on CpGs with the highest age correlation is well-calibrated to test data, outperforming published EN-trained clocks."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The biological relevance of the genes annotated to the CpGs used in our mFSS OLS model is known and their inclusion in the model is due to their age-specific change in DNA methylation. An analysis of the roles of the top CpG-annotated genes in our mFSS that overlap between the 450 K datasets, suggests that they mostly reflect the aging-related changes, such as increase in body mass/fat, repeated instances of viral infections and inflammation, pre-cancerous growths, alcohol detoxification, and changes in teeth/hair/skin. (Supplementary Excel "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM6"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"). Other notable roles include protein degradation, maturation of the extracellular matrix, and perception of pain."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec27","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Coherence theory of disease-related shifts for linear models"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"It has been suggested, without empirical verification by randomized blinded studies, that deviations between predicted and actual ages signify age acceleration ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5291","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 1","title":"Horvath S. DNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types. Genome Biol. 2013;14:115.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR1"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"1"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5294","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Hannum G, Guinney J, Zhao L, Zhang L, Hughes G, Sadda S, Klotzle B, Bibikova M, Fan J-B, Gao Y, Deconde R. Genomewide methylation profiles reveal quantitative views of human aging rates. Mol Cell. 2013;49(2):359–67.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5294_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Levine ME, Lu AT, Quach A, Chen BH, Assimes TL, Bandinelli S, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Stewart JD, Li Y, Whitsel EA. An epigenetic biomarker of aging for lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2018;10(4):573–91.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5297","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 5","title":"Lu AT, Quach A, Wilson JG, Reiner AP, Aviv A, Raj K, Hou L, Baccarelli AA, Li Y, Stewart JD, Whitsel EA. DNA methylation grimage strongly predicts lifespan and healthspan. Aging (Albany NY). 2019;11(2):303–27.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR5"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5300","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Ying K, Liu H, Tarkhov AE, Sadler MC, Lu AT, Moqri M, Horvath S, Kutalik Z, Shen X, Gladyshev VN. Causality-enriched epigenetic age uncouples damage and adaptation. Nat Aging. 2024;4:1–16.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR7"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"7"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5300_1","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Horvath S, Raj K. DNA methylation-based biomarkers and the epigenetic clock theory of ageing. Nat Rev Genet. 2018;19(6):371–84.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR8"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"8"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5300_2","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bozack AK, Rifas-Shiman SL, Gold DR, Laubach ZM, Perng W, Hivert M-F, Cardenas A. DNA methylation age at birth and childhood: performance of epigenetic clocks and characteristics associated with epigenetic age acceleration in the project viva cohort. Clin Epigenetics. 2023;15(1):62.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR9"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"9"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5300_3","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"Bell CG, Lowe R, Adams PD, Baccarelli AA, Beck S, Bell JT, Christensen BC, Gladyshev VN, Heijmans BT, Horvath S, Ideker T. DNA methylation aging clocks: challenges and recommendations. Genome Biol. 2019;20:249.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR10"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"10"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5300_4","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","title":"He X, Liu J, Liu B, Shi J. The use of DNA methylation clock in aging research. Exp Biol Med. 2021;246(4):436–46.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR11"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"11"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":","},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5303","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 12","title":"Bergsma T, Rogaeva E. DNA methylation clocks and their predictive capacity for aging phenotypes and healthspan. Neurosci Insights. 2020;15:2633105520942221.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR12"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"12"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"], yet our data shows that such deviations could be also due to random experimental variation and healthy dynamics of DNAm. Additionally, EN clocks do not reliably distinguish healthy people from patients diagnosed with diseases, including those of inflammaging (["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e5307","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 23","title":"Mei X, Blanchard J, Luellen C, Conboy MJ, Conboy IM. Fail-tests of DNA methylation clocks, and development of a noise barometer for measuring epigenetic pressure of aging and disease. Aging (Albany NY). 2023;15(17):8552.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR23"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"23"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"] and Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig2"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"2"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":")."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"At the same time, as expected, there are some CpGs that reliably detect inflammaging-specific methylation; Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a shows two such cases of rheumatoid arthritis. In solving this conundrum where inflammaging vs. health definition is low in linear models, but DNAm changes with inflammaging are pronounced in the DNAm array data, we note that both increased "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"and"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" decreased shifts in DNAm that are disease-specific are possible, and pairing those signal shifts with weights, which can be either positive or negative, in linear combination may cause a negation of the disease signal present at individual CpGs. This implies that diseases do not inherently produce an upward shift in predicted age (or age acceleration) when simply summing methylation states across CpGs in a linear model. It would be more robust and better reflect the biology to pair a positive shift with positive weight and negative with negative."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"In linear models, the signs of the weights are determined by the solution which minimizes the cost function of the model and need not depend on the signs of the correlations between individual features and the response variable (i.e., a feature with a positive association with the response variable may still be assigned a negative weight in the multiple regression solution, and vice versa). Similarly, individual feature shifts can be either positive or negative, depending on the agreement in sign between the model weights and signal shifts (Box 2, Supplementary Fig. 3b). This can cause an artificial cancellation effect and can produce a net age shift near or equal to zero despite significant differences between the methylomes of inflammaging and healthy states. We define such a property of a linear model as model "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"incoherence"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":".\n"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"table-3","attributes":{"class":"c-article-table","data-test":"inline-table","data-container-section":"table"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-table__figcaption"},"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Tabb","attributes":{"data-test":"table-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Box 2  Definitions of signal shift, age shift, feature shift and feature rectification "}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"u-text-right u-hide-print"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"table-link","data-track":"click","data-track-action":"view table","data-track-label":"button","rel":"nofollow","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/tables/3","aria-label":"Full size table 3"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size table"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Conversely, we define the property where all feature shifts agree in sign to produce a net positive age shift as model "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"coherence"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":". Due to the association of "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"overprediction"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" of age with age acceleration, we define positive feature shifts as "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"coherent"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" and negative feature shifts as "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"de-coherent"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":". The degree of incoherence in a model is expected to vary for different diseases and to depend on the interactions between the feature shifts for a given disease. Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM3"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b summarizes the interactions between the signal shifts and model weights which produce coherent and de-coherent feature shifts."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Model coherence is a desirable property if researchers would like their DNAm age predictors to not only predict chronological age, but also to detect shifted trajectories of biological age. Here, we propose a method, which we term "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"feature rectification"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", for inducing model coherence during model training by allowing only positive feature shifts. While the signs of feature weights for a model cannot be known a priori, it is possible to constrain a model to have non-negative weights, thereby eliminating de-coherent feature shifts, where signal shifts are positive."}]},{"tag":"h3","hidden":false,"id":"Sec28","attributes":{"class":"c-article__sub-heading"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"mFSS OLS for coherent, generalizable age predictor, and inflammaging detector"}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Following the success with the toy data, we tested our coherence theory for linear models by training a mFSS model and with feature rectification for actual PBMC samples from people with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and healthy controls. We calculated the RA signal shifts in the GSE42861 dataset (Supplementary Excel "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM7"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"3"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":") for the top 10,000 age-correlated CpGs (with respect to the samples in GSE40279) and performed the reflection transformation for the negatively shifted probe signals in the RA test set and the GSE40279 and GSE55763 training and validation datasets, respectively (methods). Next, we trained an mFSS OLS model constrained to have non-negative weights. The features were fed into the model ranked by the magnitude of their age correlation in the training set. The validation set MSE was optimized at the 680th feature, with 124 features having non-zero weights (selected features and weights, feature rectification information in Supplementary Excel "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM8"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", training and validation performance in Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a)."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"We next obtained age predictions for the RA test set and plotted them against the actual ages. Figure "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a compares the incoherent, basic mFSS OLS model predictions and the mFSS OLS model trained on rectified features. The coherence is apparent for the latter model, with the RA cohort predictions visibly shifted upward relative to the healthy control (HC) cohort. We quantified the shift by performing a two-tailed Welch’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"t"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-test on the distributions of the residuals of the HC and RA cohorts ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 1"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"9"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"e"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" − 25) and calculating the effect size of the shift ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"83) (Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b and c). Although the RA dataset was not used to train the model directly, the feature rectification was based on the signal shifts between the RA and HC cohorts in the GSE42861 dataset. To test whether the coherence effect is still present in completely out-of-sample data, we predicted the ages on several other datasets with different disease cohorts (multiple sclerosis, depression, Parkinson’s disease, and tauopathies) (Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b-e). Each of the disease cohorts was slightly up-shifted relative to their respective healthy control cohorts, but with very small effect sizes ranging from "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"14 to "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"19."}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-4","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item","data-test":"figure","data-container-section":"figure","data-title":"Fig. 4"},"children":[{"tag":"figure","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"figcaption","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"id":"Fig4","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-caption","data-test":"figure-caption-text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Fig. 4"}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-content"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-item"},"children":[{"tag":"picture","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-picture"},"children":[{"tag":"source","hidden":false,"attributes":{"type":"image/webp","srcset":"//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig4_HTML.png?as=webp"}},{"tag":"img","hidden":false,"attributes":{"aria-describedby":"figure-4-desc","src":"https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11357-024-01460-1/MediaObjects/11357_2024_1460_Fig4_HTML.png","alt":"Fig. 4","loading":"lazy","width":"685","height":"402"}}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-link"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article__pill-button","data-test":"article-link","data-track":"click","data-track-label":"button","data-track-action":"view figure","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1/figures/4","data-track-dest":"link:Figure4 Full size image","aria-label":"Full size image figure 4","rel":"nofollow"},"children":[{"tag":"span","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Full size image"}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"figure-4-desc","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__figure-description","data-test":"bottom-caption"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Feature-rectified coherent mFSS model detects inflammaging. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"a"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" (Left) Scatterplot of ages predicted by the basic mFSS clock vs. actual ages for the GSE42861 dataset colored by disease state (healthy controls in blue; rheumatoid arthritis in red) compared to predictions of a coherent mFSS model trained on rectified features based on RA signal shifts (right). Correlation, "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-value, and MAE calculated for the control cohort only. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"b"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Box plots of the residuals for healthy controls and rheumatoid arthritis patients corresponding to the basic mFSS model (left) and coherent mFSS model trained on rectified features (right). "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-values determined by Welch’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"t"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-test. Effect size ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":") determined by Cohen’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" (methods). "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"c"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Histograms and KDE plots, normalized by cohort size, of the residuals for healthy controls and rheumatoid arthritis patients corresponding to the basic mFSS model (left) and coherent mFSS model trained on rectified features (right). Vertical lines indicate the cohort means. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Scatter plot of predicted age vs. actual age for a combined dataset (GSE42861, GSE72774, GSE106648) colored by disease state (healthy controls in blue; inflammaging-associated disease in red), compared to predictions of a coherent mFSS model trained on rectified features based on inflammaging signal shifts (right). Correlation, "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-value, and MAE were calculated for the control cohort only. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"e"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Box plots of the residuals corresponding to the basic mFSS model (left) and coherent mFSS model (IR-mFSS model) trained on rectified features (right) on the combined dataset. "},{"tag":"b","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"f"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" Histograms and KDE plots, normalized by cohort size, of the residuals for the basic mFSS model (left) and coherent mFSS model (IR-mFSS model) trained on rectified features (right) on the combined dataset. Vertical lines indicate the cohort means"}]}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Interestingly, training on features ranked instead by the magnitude of the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"signal shifts"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" between healthy controls and RA reduced the accuracy of predictions of chronological age in all datasets and decreased calibration, while increasing the resolution between health and inflammaging for all but one disease (Parkinson’s) (Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM12"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b-f). The MSE for the validation set was optimized at the 1656th feature with 47 features having non-zero weights (Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM12"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a). This is expected, because to linearly predict age with the lowest error, the weights of the CpGs that change the most with inflammaging between people of the "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"same age"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" will be minimized via the least squares constraint."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Next, to make a more generalized inflammaging-coherent model, we trained a mFSS OLS model with feature rectification based on a combined dataset with inflammaging-associated disease cohorts (GSE42861, GSE106648, GSE72774), which we dubbed the inflammaging-rectified mFSS (IR-mFSS) model. The model was again trained on GSE40279 and validated on GSE55763, with the top 10,000 age-correlated CpGs (per the GSE40279 samples) ranked by absolute signal shift magnitude (Supplementary Excel "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM9"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"5"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":") for the combined inflammaging dataset (selected features and weights, feature rectification information in Supplementary Excel 6, training and validation performance in Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM13"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"6"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"a). Figure "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"d compares the incoherent basic mFSS OLS model predictions on the combined inflammaging dataset and the coherent IR-mFSS model. We quantified the shift by performing a two-tailed Welch’s "},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"t"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"-test on the distributions of the residuals of the HC and inflammaging cohorts ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"p"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 6"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"1"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"e"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" − 35) and calculating the effect size, which was medium-to-large ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0"},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"65), as seen in Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"figure anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#Fig4"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"4"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"e and f. Supplementary Fig. "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM13"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"6"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"b-f show the distributions of the residuals for different datasets, comparing patients with inflammaging-associated diseases and healthy controls. The IR-mFSS clock increased the resolution over the basic mFSS clock and substantially for all diseases, with effect sizes ranging from small ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 0.27) to very large ("},{"tag":"i","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"d"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" = 1.3)."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The genes annotated to the 47 CpGs selected by the IR-mFSS clock and their functions are provided in (Supplementary Excel "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-label":"link","data-track-action":"supplementary material anchor","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#MOESM11"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"7"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":")."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"While EN regression is not essential for creating an accurate and generalizable age predictor, an interesting avenue for future research would be exploring how EN would assign weights if trained only on data over the feature space of the IR-mFSS CpG selection. It would also be interesting to see the influence of EN on the interpretability and disease detection capabilities of our model’s feature selection."}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Discussion"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec29-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"Sec29","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Discussion"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Sec29-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"This study establishes why linear machine learning models, such as EN clocks, underperform in their ability to distinguish health from inflammaging. Specifically, we show that linear EN regression—whether trained to predict age, health scores, mortality, or other outcomes— consistently produces feature selections lacking clear biological meaning in all major DNA clocks. The features selected may simply minimize the EN cost function. Our results show that CpG importance in the DNAm clocks is assigned in disregard of the age-specific changes in DNAm; it remains to be seen what biological process, if any, plays a role in the assigned weights, or if the subsets are simply a mathematical artifact of model optimization and batch effect."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"We also determined that many of the EN-trained models are poorly calibrated for out-of-sample data and have very large natural variances in prediction errors for healthy people. Thus, there is no reason to think about loss of health or age acceleration unless a residual is more than 10–20 years, and until a blinded randomized study empirically verifies the predictive accuracy of a given model."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"In our study, we highlight the need for a compromise in feature selection. CpGs that correlate with chronological age are crucial for robust age predictions, but for effective disease detection—as we have demonstrated with inflammaging-associated diseases—it is equally important to include CpGs whose methylation states change with disease (and which may or may not correlate with chronological age). Moreover, our results demonstrate such CpGs can be included without a significant decrease in the accuracy of age prediction. Previous clock models primarily focus on CpGs that correlate with time progression and do not intentionally incorporate disease-related CpGs, which limits their ability to detect deviations from health, such as those associated with inflammaging. Our approach deliberately includes these disease-associated CpGs, which enhances the model’s ability to both predict chronological age accurately in the absence of inflammaging and detect inflammaging through positive shifts in predicted age. This trade-off reflects the competing goals of optimizing both age prediction and disease detection, which cannot be maximized simultaneously in a single model."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"To enable researchers with the ability to incorporate specific, ranked subsets of CpGs and improve the biological relevance of DNAm age predictors, we developed a modified forward stepwise selection OLS regression model with a clear hypothesis-driven feature selection. We demonstrated that such mFSS models are ML-independent yet are generalizable and better calibrated than EN clocks when trained on features with at least some correlation with age."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Importantly, we identified a key detriment of linear models when using residuals as a measure of age acceleration, namely, an incoherence between combinations of multiple CpG features, some of which shift in their biological DNAm in an opposite direction of the model-assigned weights. Our theory for linear model coherence explains why linear models tend not to provide resolution between health and inflammaging despite significant disease-imposed changes in CpG beta values. This theory is confirmed by the demonstration that feature rectification yields coherent, generalizable linear models through mFSS OLS, which can detect aging and simultaneously, inflammaging. Of note, while feature rectification provides a necessary correction to enable linear models to detect inflammaging neither biological aging nor most diseases are linear processes, so interpretation of apparent differences in aging trajectories between healthy and patients’ cohorts should be made with caution."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"In our study, we focused on inflammation-related diseases because they present a unique challenge for DNA methylation clock models. Inflammation-related diseases are characterized by age-associated, systemic inflammatory changes that often lead to deviations in methylation patterns not strictly aligned with chronological aging. These inflammatory changes, driven by both intrinsic aging and extrinsic health factors, offer an ideal test case for our method’s ability to detect disease-related shifts in DNAm."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"However, we believe that our approach has the potential to be generalized to other diseases, although we anticipate that the heterogeneity of diseases—especially those with less direct connections to aging processes—might introduce additional complexities. Diseases with varying methylation signatures may require even more tailored feature selection or rectification methods to reliably detect deviations from health. For diseases with more uniform effects on methylation, such as those strongly linked to specific genes or pathways, our approach could be adapted with only moderate adjustments. In contrast, highly heterogeneous diseases might face an information limit if the diversity of CpG methylation changes dilutes the signal for any specific disease state."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"In sum, while inflammaging was an ideal starting point for our method, we acknowledge that the applicability to other diseases will depend on the disease’s specific impact on methylation patterns and may require disease-specific adjustments to achieve optimal detection accuracy. We also foresee the potential for a more general disease predictor that incorporates various disease-specific CpG sites, which could serve as indicators—contributing to positive shifts in predicted age in the presence of disease while having minimal influence on age predictions in healthy individuals."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"While our model demonstrates accuracy in predicting chronological age and detecting inflammaging, it remains a linear model and is thus limited in its ability to capture non-linear relationships between CpG sites and complex aging or health dynamics. Consequently, this restricts the model’s ability to fully reflect biological age, as it may miss nuanced, multi-dimensional dependencies essential for capturing the biological intricacies of aging and disease progression."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The genes linked to the CpGs selected by our IR-mFSS OLS model largely align with well-established biological functions relevant to aging, including immune response, lipid metabolism, DNA damage response, and neurodevelopment. However, we also identified several genes that may represent novel findings and warrant further exploration."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"One such gene is FAM86A (FAM86C2P), which is a pseudogene. Although pseudogenes were once considered non-functional, recent studies have shown that some pseudogenes play important regulatory roles, particularly in gene expression and epigenetic processes ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6559","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 33","title":"Grander D, Johnsson P: Pseudogene-expressed RNAs: emerging roles in gene regulation and disease. Long Non-coding RNAs Human Dis. (2015) 111–126.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR33"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"33"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. According to Gene Ontology (GO) annotations on GeneCards and NCBI, FAM86C2P is predicted to have methyltransferase activity, which could suggest a potential role in DNA methylation processes. However, it is important to note that this function has not yet been experimentally validated (GeneCards, NCBI Gene ID: 645,332) ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6562","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 34","title":"GeneCards: FAM86C2P gene (Family With Sequence Similarity 86 Member C2, Pseudogene). Accessed: 2024–11–11 (2023). \n                  https://www.genecards.org/cgi-bin/carddisp.pl?gene=FAM86C2P\n                  \n                ","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR34"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"34"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6565","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 35","title":"NCBI Gene: Gene ID: 645332, FAM86C2P. Accessed: 2024–11–11 (2023). \n                  https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene/645332\n                  \n                ","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR35"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"35"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. This opens the possibility that FAM86A could have a regulatory role in the aging process that warrants further investigation, particularly in the context of age-related epigenetic changes."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Additionally, we found SYNGAP1, a gene primarily involved in neurodevelopment, but also neuroplasticity, which may link it to neurodegenerative processes ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6571","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 36","title":"Jeyabalan N, Clement JP. Syngap1: mind the gap. Front Cell Neurosci. 2016;10:32.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR36"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"36"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6574","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 37","title":"Clement JP, Aceti M, Creson TK, Ozkan ED, Shi Y, Reish NJ, Almonte AG, Miller BH, Wiltgen BJ, Miller CA, et al. Pathogenic syngap1 mutations impair cognitive development by disrupting maturation of dendritic spine synapses. Cell. 2012;151(4):709–23.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR37"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"37"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]. Its inclusion in the model suggests that neurodevelopmental genes could have broader implications for aging-related decline in cognitive function or other neurodegenerative diseases."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The inclusion of long non-coding RNAs like LOC285954 and SLC22A18AS also presents a novel angle, as this class of genes is gaining attention for its potential role in regulating aging processes, yet their function in aging is not yet fully understood ["},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6580","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 38","title":"Muro EM, Mah N, Andrade-Navarro MA. Functional evidence of post-transcriptional regulation by pseudogenes. Biochimie. 2011;93(11):1916–21.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR38"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"38"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":", "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"id":"ref-link-section-d40099685e6583","attributes":{"data-track":"click","data-track-action":"reference anchor","data-track-label":"link","data-test":"citation-ref","aria-label":"Reference 39","title":"Milligan MJ, Lipovich L. Pseudogene-derived lncRNAs: emerging regulators of gene expression. Front Genet. 2015;5:476.","href":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11357-024-01460-1#ref-CR39"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"39"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"]."}]}]}]}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                                "}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                        \n                    \n\n                        \n\n                    \n                        \n                    \n\n                    \n\n                    "},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Data availability"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"data-availability-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"data-availability","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Data availability"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"data-availability-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n              \n              "},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"All data used in this study was downloaded from the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) repository: "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"href":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n              "},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Raw IDAT files were downloaded and preprocessed (methods) when available. For all others, the processed datasets were downloaded."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]}]}]},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Datasets used"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"datasets-used-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"datasets-used","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Datasets used"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"datasets-used-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n              \n              "},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"The datasets used are as follows: GSE42861, GSE125105, GSE40279, GSE55763, GSE106648, GSE72774, GSE53740."}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]}]}]},{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-title":"Code availability"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"code-availability-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"code-availability","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Code availability"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"code-availability-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n              \n              "},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"All relevant code can be accessed at "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"href":"https://github.com/SkinnerCM/conboy-laboratory/tree/main/dnam-clocks/mFSS-clock"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"https://github.com/SkinnerCM/conboy-laboratory/tree/main/dnam-clocks/mFSS-clock"}]}]},{"tag":"#text","text":"\n            "}]}]}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"MagazineFulltextArticleBodySuffix","children":[{"tag":"section","hidden":false,"attributes":{"aria-labelledby":"Bib1","data-title":"References"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Bib1-section","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section"},"children":[{"tag":"h2","hidden":false,"id":"Bib1","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"References"}]},{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"id":"Bib1-content","attributes":{"class":"c-article-section__content"},"children":[{"tag":"div","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-container-section":"references"},"children":[{"tag":"ol","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-references","data-track-component":"outbound reference","data-track-context":"references section"},"children":[{"tag":"li","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__item js-c-reading-companion-references-item","data-counter":"1."},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"id":"ref-CR1","attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Horvath S. DNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types. Genome Biol. 2013;14:115."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__links u-hide-print"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click_references","rel":"noopener","data-track-label":"10.1186/gb-2013-14-10-r115","data-track-item_id":"10.1186/gb-2013-14-10-r115","data-track-value":"article reference","data-track-action":"article reference","href":"https://link.springer.com/doi/10.1186/gb-2013-14-10-r115","aria-label":"Article reference 1","data-doi":"10.1186/gb-2013-14-10-r115"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Article"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" \n    "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click_references","data-track-action":"google scholar reference","data-track-value":"google scholar reference","data-track-label":"link","data-track-item_id":"link","rel":"nofollow noopener","aria-label":"Google Scholar reference 1","href":"http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=DNA%20methylation%20age%20of%20human%20tissues%20and%20cell%20types&journal=Genome%20Biol&doi=10.1186%2Fgb-2013-14-10-r115&volume=14&publication_year=2013&author=Horvath%2CS"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                    Google Scholar"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" \n                "}]}]},{"tag":"li","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__item js-c-reading-companion-references-item","data-counter":"2."},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"id":"ref-CR2","attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Zou H, Hastie T. Regularization and variable selection via the elastic net. J R Stat Soc Ser B Stat Methodol. 2005;67(2):301–20."}]},{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__links u-hide-print"},"children":[{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click_references","rel":"nofollow noopener","data-track-label":"10.1111/j.1467-9868.2005.00503.x","data-track-item_id":"10.1111/j.1467-9868.2005.00503.x","data-track-value":"article reference","data-track-action":"article reference","href":"https://doi.org/10.1111%2Fj.1467-9868.2005.00503.x","aria-label":"Article reference 2","data-doi":"10.1111/j.1467-9868.2005.00503.x"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"Article"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" \n    "},{"tag":"a","hidden":false,"attributes":{"data-track":"click_references","data-track-action":"google scholar reference","data-track-value":"google scholar reference","data-track-label":"link","data-track-item_id":"link","rel":"nofollow noopener","aria-label":"Google Scholar reference 2","href":"http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=Regularization%20and%20variable%20selection%20via%20the%20elastic%20net&journal=J%20R%20Stat%20Soc%20Ser%20B%20Stat%20Methodol&doi=10.1111%2Fj.1467-9868.2005.00503.x&volume=67&issue=2&pages=301-320&publication_year=2005&author=Zou%2CH&author=Hastie%2CT"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","text":"\n                    Google Scholar"}]},{"tag":"#text","text":" \n                "}]}]},{"tag":"li","hidden":false,"attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__item js-c-reading-companion-references-item","data-counter":"3."},"children":[{"tag":"p","hidden":false,"id":"ref-CR3","attributes":{"class":"c-article-references__text"},"children":[{"tag":"#text","